 ## Background
-Visibility culling in volume rendering has evolved from simple geometric checks to complex, view-dependent, and feedback-driven mechanisms. Early implementations focused on reducing overhead in ray casting by dividing image space into screen regions to skip full areas [S1, unofficial]. As rendering moved to GPU-based pipelines, techniques like scalar range lookup tables were introduced to cull cells based on transfer function opacity ranges, significantly improving performance for AMR datasets [S2]. More recent developments incorporate view-dependent preprocessing, such as 24-region visibility masks, and feedback loops that use screen-space subdivision to manage out-of-core data loading [S1, unofficial]. The family also includes safety mechanisms like interactive render quality estimation to prevent infinite loops in real-time applications [S1, unofficial].
